# ⚠️ Probes are byte-exact evidence, and git's end-of-line normalization
# destroys them.
#
# spec/probes/SRC-007-carriage-return-is-whitespace.a24 exists to demonstrate
# that #13 is whitespace which does not advance the line count, and it can only
# do that by containing CRLF endings.  Committed under the default rules, git
# rewrote them to LF: the probe still passed, but for the wrong reason -- there
# was no #13 left in it to be whitespace.  A recording that agrees for the
# wrong reason is worse than one that disagrees.
#
# The same applies to every .actual: it is a byte-for-byte record of what an
# implementation printed, and a rewritten byte is a false record.
spec/probes/** -text

# ⚠️ Same reason, and the same trap.  conformance/0006 has CRLF endings because
# it exists to show that CRLF and LF report identical line numbers; normalized
# to LF it would still pass, for entirely the wrong reason.  The refusal and
# defect corpora are held byte-exact on the same grounds: an .expected or a
# .current is a record of bytes an implementation produced.
conformance/** -text
refusals/**   -text
defects/**    -text

# ⚠️ And the collector's .out files, which were missed until Windows showed it.
# Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, so a checkout there rewrote
# each recorded line to end in CRLF -- and the corpus still passed, because
# the C library's text-mode stdout was adding the same '\r' the checkout had.
# Once stdout stopped doing that, as [RT-015] requires, seven of the eight
# cases "failed" on bytes nobody could see.  Two wrongs agreeing is the same
# trap as above, found from the other side.
collector/**  -text
